{"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are ThreadSense grounding verifier. You verify independently of the drafting agent; you judge and never repair.\nYour personal goal is: Judge every statement against the full topic context and decide whether the passed premises justify the exact proposed action."},{"role":"user","content":"\nCurrent Task: Judge each statement against the evidence it cites, read within the full context provided: every message of every member thread (with canonical author IDs, roles, thread starters and reactions) and every retrieved source for this topic. Citing a source is not support. A statement is SUPPORTED only if its cited evidence states or directly entails it and nothing in the context contradicts it; PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED if a material part is missing or the context qualifies it; UNSUPPORTED otherwise. A status line marking an excerpt historical, unresolved or a prior record means it cannot support what applies now. By kind: CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT is a report of what members say, not a verified cause; CLAIM_SOURCE_FACT is SUPPORTED if the cited source says it, described with its status and dates; it establishes no current handling, procedure or destination; CLAIM_CAUSE needs verification in the cited evidence; a Verbatim quote must match its source, a Paraphrase must preserve the meaning. Prose names no one: role wording (\"the team lead instructs\", \"a tutor reports\") must match the cited author's role and act (reported, asked, confirmed or instructed), plural wording needs that many distinct cited authors, and listed attributions must hold (AUTHOR wrote the cited message, THREAD_STARTER started its thread, PARTICIPANT posted in it). A retrieved record's authorship is separate from its authority: an approver or recorder (actor_relation RECORD_AUTHORITY) is not its author, and a record whose authorship is not verified names no author. SUMMARY and SEVERITY_RATIONALE must follow from the evidence with no added cause, count or outcome. A SUMMARY sentence (statement_id summary:s1, summary:s2, ...) is judged on its own and shown only if SUPPORTED; its attributions name the people its role wording refers to, and wording that groups several people is SUPPORTED only if what it says they asked or reported is stated in each of their cited messages. A SUMMARY sentence with depends_on is read after the sentences it lists (for words such as \"this\" or \"their\"), but judged only on what it adds. A DETAIL sentence (statement_id detail:d1, detail:d2, ...) is shown when the card is opened and is judged exactly like a SUMMARY sentence: on its own, shown only if SUPPORTED, read after the sentences in its depends_on but judged only on what it adds. NO_ACTION_RATIONALE is the advisor's reason for proposing no further intervention now: SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence shows that reason and the context shows no unresolved handling question, guidance gap, applicable pending decision or operational problem that needs coordination; existing guidance does not by itself establish that no action is needed. ACTION and ALTERNATIVE are recommendations: judge whether their premises are supported, whether the action applies to this topic and whether the rationale follows; a source need not literally prescribe the action. Recipients and routes: keep apart what an action recommends, the functional recipient it proposes (engineering, a specialist, a QA reviewer) and any concrete route it names (a specific thread, channel, person or queue it goes to or through). A proposed functional recipient is justified by supported incident evidence showing a problem that needs that recipient's expertise; evidence of an established intake route is not required, and mentioning the recipient justifies nothing by itself. A concrete route, or a claim that a route is designated, official or mandatory, is a claim of its own and needs a premise whose evidence establishes it; an example-collection or status note asks people where to post examples and does not make that thread an intake route. The recommendation stays advisory (the team lead decides; it need not be shown to be the required or only next step): when no evidence names the concrete route, leave it unspecified (\"report it to engineering\") rather than choosing one. Judge a proposed recipient the same way in ACTION, ALTERNATIVE, INTERVENTION_NEED, ACTION_RATIONALE and REVIEW_NOTE: on whether the incident evidence shows a problem that needs that recipient's expertise, never on whether an intake route is on record; a statement that proposes a recipient and leaves the concrete route unspecified is not PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED, UNSUPPORTED or insufficient for that reason. ACTION and operational ALTERNATIVE statements list their premises: PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT premises are judged here and are provisional, not final; REJECTED ones failed deterministic checks and support nothing; a premise with depends_on stands only if every ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING statement it lists is also SUPPORTED. ACTION_CHOICE is SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence really leaves that choice between the named actions open (not merely an imagined alternative, a review flag or an unknown cause). INTERVENTION_NEED says why intervening now is needed: SUPPORTED only if the context shows that reason (an unresolved handling question, a guidance gap, an applicable pending decision, or an operational problem that needs coordination). Questions already answered in their threads are not unresolved; a general benefit (\"a reminder might help\") is not a need; existing guidance does not by itself rule a need out. Judge in this order: (1) announcement handling; (2) each premise's final status, which is your verdict on it combined with its dependencies; (3) sufficiency. Fill action_sufficiency for the ACTION and one alternative_sufficiency entry (with its statement_id) for each ALTERNATIVE that lists premises: SUFFICIENT only if premises whose final status is SUPPORTED justify the exact text as worded, including its conditions and scope; essential_statement_ids lists the premises it cannot stand without (each must have final status SUPPORTED); INSUFFICIENT if its exact text relies on a rejected, unsupported or dependency-voided premise or goes beyond its premises; a listed premise the text does not rely on is not essential, and its failure alone does not make the action insufficient. ACTION_RATIONALE is judged separately and may also cite context claims; action_sufficiency null if no ACTION statement is sent. SOURCE_AGREEMENT is SUPPORTED only if the named current sources say the same thing on the point in conflict and only the superseded material differs. A request by anyone but a team lead is a CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT: SUPPORTED if that person asked it, and it carries no authority. CLAIM_THREAD_INSTRUCTION is a team lead's in-thread instruction: SUPPORTED only if that message gives it and it applies to the threads in scope; it is not project guidance. HISTORICAL_RECURRENCE needs retrieved records of earlier occurrences; REPORTED_RECURRENCE only needs members saying it is happening again. AFFECTED_REPORTER needs that person's own message reporting that they are affected. RESOLUTION is judged against the member's complete thread, including messages the statement did not cite. ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING needs the announcement to give that directive for this topic's project, batch and problem, and it governs only what it directs (a request to post examples governs where examples go, not escalation or an intake route); a routine reminder or schedule directs nothing for the topic. OPEN_QUESTION is SUPPORTED only if the guidance and decisions in the context really leave that specific question unsettled and it is a handling or policy question for the team lead; a not-yet-diagnosed cause, pending diagnostic results or a pending expert assessment is not one. Return exactly one verdict for every statement_id. The team lead reads SUMMARY, DETAIL, ACTION (label, instruction, draft to copy, scope and changes), ACTION_RATIONALE, NO_ACTION_RATIONALE, REVIEW_NOTE and OPEN_QUESTION exactly as written: judge that exact text, including its conditions, uncertainty, scope and who reported, asked, confirmed or instructed; plain wording is expected, but it must not overstate the evidence. A quoted passage must match its source exactly. open_review_flags lists the upstream review obligations still open on these threads; a REVIEW_NOTE may rely on them. Source consistency (always fill source_consistency, even when the draft declares no conflict): compare the governing_sources that address the handling question of this topic with each other, independently of the draft. List sources that state the same rule as AGREE, and sources that state different rules for the same cases as CONFLICT with the point and disagreement_scope (which cases, batch or section differ). assessed_evidence_ids lists the governing sources you compared; relations may be empty when they address different points. Only governing sources count: historical, superseded, expired or withdrawn material, prior records and member messages are not current sources, an announcement governs only if it directs handling for this topic, and members disagreeing with each other is not a source conflict. For each entry in declared_conflicts give one declared_conflict_checks entry: CONFIRMED (the cited sources state different rules on that point), SOURCES_AGREE (they say the same thing; only wording or detail differs) or UNDETERMINED.\n\nInput JSON:\n{\"topic\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"batch_id\": \"safety_batch_08\", \"candidate_group_id\": \"cluster_57ca38768e3b8274\", \"topic_id\": \"cluster_57ca38768e3b8274/topic_1\", \"topic_label\": \"Consistent threshold for comparable borderline cases\", \"projection_gap\": null, \"as_of\": \"2026-10-02T12:00:00Z\", \"member_ids\": [\"member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920\", \"member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\"]}, \"statements\": [{\"statement_id\": \"claim:c1\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor reports that a few L0s classify borderline items more strictly.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U015\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c2\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor reports that two reviewers assigned different labels to the same contextual passage.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U013\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c3\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor says the first thread's example has the same relevant context across both submissions and recommends applying the same safety threshold while recording genuine differences explicitly.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c4\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor reports opposing safety labels from two QA passes for nearly the same case and asks for the correct handling of SAFETY-7526.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U003\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c5\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor reports seeing opposite QA reasons for closely matched prompts and asks for both rationales before calling the issue QA drift.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U020\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c6\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor asks whether the examples in the SAFETY-7526 thread are truly comparable in intent and explicitness.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U019\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c7\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor answers the SAFETY-7526 handling question by saying the submissions share the same relevant context and recommending the same safety threshold, with genuine differences recorded explicitly.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U014\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c8\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_GUIDANCE\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ directs reviewers to read surrounding meaning and purpose rather than classify only from matching keywords; it does not assess the comparability of the reported cases.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s1\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"Three tutors report conflicting labels or QA reasons for the same passage or closely matched cases across two threads.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U013\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U003\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U020\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s2\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor in each thread answered the handling issue by recommending the same safety threshold for submissions sharing the same relevant context, with genuine differences recorded explicitly.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U014\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d1\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"A tutor also reports that a few L0s classify borderline items more strictly.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U015\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d2\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"For SAFETY-7526, a tutor asks how to handle opposing safety labels from two QA passes.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U003\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d3\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"In the SAFETY-7526 thread, a tutor asks for both rationales before calling the issue QA drift, and a QA tutor asks whether the examples are comparable in intent and explicitness.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U020\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U019\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d4\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ says to consider surrounding meaning and purpose rather than classify only from matching keywords; it does not establish that these particular cases are comparable.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"action:no_action\", \"kind\": \"NO_ACTION_RATIONALE\", \"text\": \"Why no further action is needed now: A QA tutor has answered the handling issue in each thread. Neither thread shows a remaining handling question after that answer that requires a further intervention now.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"resolution:member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920\", \"kind\": \"RESOLUTION\", \"text\": \"Member thread member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920 is RESOLVED_IN_THREAD: A QA tutor addresses the reported inconsistency with a case-specific answer about using the same threshold and recording genuine differences. The messages do not confirm that existing labels were corrected.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"resolution:member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\", \"kind\": \"RESOLUTION\", \"text\": \"Member thread member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129 is RESOLVED_IN_THREAD: A QA tutor answers the task-handling question with a case-specific same-threshold recommendation after the comparability question. This resolves the handling question in the thread, without verifying QA drift or a correction to saved labels.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"severity:rationale\", \"kind\": \"SEVERITY_RATIONALE\", \"text\": \"Severity HIGH: Three tutors report comparable label or QA-reason disagreements across two threads, indicating a broader labeling-quality risk even though both threads have handling answers. This does not establish systemic QA drift or a verified cause.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"severity:signal:HIGH[3]\", \"kind\": \"SEVERITY_SIGNAL\", \"text\": \"Rubric signal met - \\\"Multiple distinct people or affected tasks reporting a comparable problem within the relevant project, batch, and time window, suggesting broader disruption or systemic labeling risk. Consider the number affected relative to the known active group when that information is available; do not require an explicit statement of widespread impact\\\": Three distinct tutors report different labels for the same passage, opposing QA labels for nearly the same case, or opposite QA reasons for closely matched prompts in two threads on the same day.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"reporter:U013\", \"kind\": \"AFFECTED_REPORTER\", \"text\": \"U013 reports, in their own cited message, being affected by this problem.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"reporter:U003\", \"kind\": \"AFFECTED_REPORTER\", \"text\": \"U003 reports, in their own cited message, being affected by this problem.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"reporter:U020\", \"kind\": \"AFFECTED_REPORTER\", \"text\": \"U020 reports, in their own cited message, being affected by this problem.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\"]}], \"member_threads\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908710.000091\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920\", \"thread_id\": \"1790908710.000091\", \"author_id\": \"U015\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T02:38:30.000091Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U015\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"A few L0s classify borderline items more strictly.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908767.000113\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920\", \"thread_id\": \"1790908710.000091\", \"author_id\": \"U013\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T02:39:27.000113Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U015\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"reactions\": [{\"name\": \"thinking_face\", \"users\": [\"U010\"]}], \"excerpt\": \"Two reviewers assigned different labels to the same contextual passage.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790908804.000226\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_db01803c1f63832c65e0b920\", \"thread_id\": \"1790908710.000091\", \"author_id\": \"U007\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T02:40:04.000226Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U015\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"reactions\": [{\"name\": \"white_check_mark\", \"users\": [\"U006\"]}], \"excerpt\": \"For this example the two submissions share the same relevant context, so apply the same safety threshold. Record any genuine difference explicitly.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927526.000088\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\", \"thread_id\": \"1790927526.000088\", \"author_id\": \"U003\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T07:52:06.000088Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U003\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"[Task review]\\nIssue: Two QA passes gave opposing safety labels for nearly the same case.\\nQuestion: what is the correct handling for this task? (task ref SAFETY-7526)\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927581.000113\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\", \"thread_id\": \"1790927526.000088\", \"author_id\": \"U020\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T07:53:01.000113Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U003\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"I am seeing opposite QA reasons for closely matched prompts. We need both rationales before saying this is QA drift.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927623.000226\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\", \"thread_id\": \"1790927526.000088\", \"author_id\": \"U019\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T07:53:43.000226Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U003\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Are the examples truly comparable in intent and explicitness?\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790927670.000339\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_693e6f3f9ca2ecb7d4551129\", \"thread_id\": \"1790927526.000088\", \"author_id\": \"U014\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T07:54:30.000339Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U003\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"reactions\": [{\"name\": \"white_check_mark\", \"users\": [\"U006\"]}], \"excerpt\": \"For this example the two submissions share the same relevant context, so apply the same safety threshold. Record any genuine difference explicitly.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}], \"retrieved_sources\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:3008b16b\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-classification-instructions@1.4\", \"title\": \"Safety Classification — Instructions (v1.4)\", \"sections\": [\"Classification and handling guide\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-classification-instructions\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"guideline\", \"record_kind\": \"guideline\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Classification and handling guide\\nThis table concerns content categories and handling. It does not assign operational HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW severity or treat acceptable content as a minor violation.\\n\\n| Area | Evidence requiring further safety review | Context that alone does not establish a violation/QN trigger |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Harmful assistance | Actual instructions or endorsement of harm | A warning or supportive quotation without harmful assistance |\\n| Privacy / PII | Identifiable private individual information | Verified fictional placeholders or official business contact information |\\n| Minor-related content | Sexualization or endangerment of a minor | A harmless contextual mention of a child |\\n| Override / jailbreak language | Actual harmful or actionable bypass content | A statement boasting about ignoring rules without corresponding harmful content |\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:4a0a4f89\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-classification-instructions@1.4\", \"title\": \"Safety Classification — Instructions (v1.4)\", \"sections\": [\"Tool incidents and submission\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-classification-instructions\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"guideline\", \"record_kind\": \"guideline\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Tool incidents and submission\\nIf the response does not load, report a response-fetch incident and leave content classification unassessed. Do not invent a safety label for unseen output.\\n\\nFor submission failures, captur